10. What is Master-Slave Replication?
Working:
One node is designated as Master (primary). All write operations go to the Master.
Changes are asynchronously (or synchronously) propagated to one or more Slave (secondary) nodes.
Clients can read from any Slave (or the Master).
Advantages:
Simple to implement.
Read scalability (add more slaves).
Master can focus on writes.
Limitations:
Master is a single point of failure for writes.
Replication lag can cause stale reads.
Write throughput limited by the Master.
Suitable applications: Read-heavy applications (news websites, content delivery, reporting systems).
11. Explain Peer-to-Peer Replication. How is it different from Master-Slave?
Peer-to-Peer Replication: Every node can accept both reads and writes. Data is replicated to other nodes. Conflict resolution mechanisms (last-write-wins, vector clocks, etc.) are used.
Differences from Master-Slave:
No single master → no single point of failure for writes.
Higher write availability and scalability.
More complex conflict resolution and consistency management.
Typically used in systems that need continuous availability (Cassandra, Riak).
12. What is Sharding?
Sharding is the process of horizontally partitioning data across multiple servers (shards). Each shard holds a subset of the data.
How data is distributed:
A shard key is chosen (e.g., userId, hash of key, range of values).
The system routes requests to the correct shard based on the shard key.
Common strategies: Range-based, Hash-based, Directory-based.
Advantages:
Horizontal scalability.
Parallel processing of queries.
Better performance for large datasets.
Challenges:
Choosing a good shard key is critical (hot spots).
Cross-shard queries/joins are expensive or impossible.
Rebalancing data when adding/removing nodes.
Increased operational complexity.
13. Combining Sharding and Replication
Architecture (conceptual):
Data is first sharded across multiple shard groups. Each shard is then replicated (usually 3 replicas).
[ Clients ]
                         |
              +----------+----------+
              |                     |
         [ Shard 1 ]           [ Shard 2 ]
         /   |   \             /   |   \
   Replica1 Replica2 Replica3  R1   R2   R3
Working:
A write is sent to the primary replica of the relevant shard.
The write is then replicated to the other replicas of that shard.
Reads can be served by any replica of the correct shard.
This provides both scalability (sharding) and high availability (replication).
14. What is MapReduce? Explain Map and Reduce phases with a simple example
MapReduce is a programming model for processing large datasets in parallel across a distributed cluster.
Map Phase:
Takes input data and emits intermediate key-value pairs.
Example (word count on documents):
Input: “apple banana apple”
Map output: (apple,1), (banana,1), (apple,1)
Reduce Phase:
Takes all values for the same key and aggregates them.
Reduce for key “apple”: values [1,1] → output (apple, 2)
Reduce for key “banana”: values [1] → output (banana, 1)
Simple database example: Counting number of orders per customer from a large orders collection.
15. Role of MapReduce in database processing and large-scale data analysis
Role in database processing:
Enables distributed processing of data that is too large for a single machine.
Used for aggregation, filtering, transformation, and complex analytics that cannot be expressed efficiently in simple queries.
Many NoSQL systems (MongoDB, HBase, Cassandra via Hadoop) support MapReduce or similar frameworks (Spark is often preferred today).
Large-scale data analysis:
Processes petabytes of data by dividing work across hundreds or thousands of nodes.
Fault-tolerant (failed tasks are re-executed).
Ideal for batch analytics: log analysis, recommendation generation, ETL pipelines, statistical computations, etc.
